#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180501} :activity/id #uuid "912b55f4-8877-5aff-bc43-d73698914e66" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049346 :topic/id #uuid "ed9d339c-fde4-545b-86b4-381d2f85203f" :topic/math-academy-id 3704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180386 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180678 :tutorial/content "Since there is a fundamental link between line integrals of vector-valued functions and line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y,$ it should be unsurprising that they share similar properties. We list two of them below:\n\n- If $-C$ denotes the path obtained by reversing the orientation of $C,$ then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\n- If $C = C_1\\cup C_2$ is the union of two paths $C_1$ and $C_2$, then\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r \n= \n\\int\\limits_{C_1} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r + \\int\\limits_{C_2} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$\n\nThe first property has an intuitive meaning if we think about it in terms of the work done by a force on a particle. To see this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. Let $C$ be the path defined by traversing the line segment from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4f/4f69f80d98d301410908f7ec867a89bdc834c22a51f224d9b00b80ceca39ab87.png)\n\n \n\nLet $W(C)$ be the work done in moving the particle along $C.$ Since our particle moves in the same direction as the vector field, we have\n\n$$\nW(C) = \\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if we move the particle from $B$ to $A,$ we expect the line integral to be negative. This is because the particle is now moving against $\\mathbf F.$ \n\n![](images/fe/fe6b2e67d33a4993104406c88b1dc0dc897be33d034007ed581a75f741ddf620.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW(-C) = \\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$\n\nWe can show that the work done in moving the particle along $-C$ is the same as the work done in moving it along $C$ but with the opposite sign. That is\n\n$$\nW(-C) = -W(C).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_{-C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r = -\\int\\limits_{C} \\mathbf F \\cdot \\mathrm d \\mathbf r.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d745eed3-7e69-55af-b685-1c7482dfffb9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3976 :tutorial/title "Reversing the Orientation of a Curve and Sums Over Unions of Paths"} :step/id #uuid "b7ff2ff0-c95d-5155-89fb-bfd7eb079783" :step/math-academy-id 23622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180390}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179669 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179524 :question/id #uuid "734d74ca-26cf-57d3-b3dc-fec66d7e0264" :question/math-academy-id "e-6286"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e5ce5418-6b75-5efa-b7f9-99ee16c510be" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048916}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180175} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345284} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Line Integral of a Vector-Valued Function Along a Union of Curves"} :step/id #uuid "5214fa4f-2dd0-5f35-827a-aa639d600260" :step/math-academy-id 23623} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180390 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179672 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179527 :question/id #uuid "2e6103cc-7be6-5eb6-ab5a-aa5aff3fa423" :question/math-academy-id "e-6319"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "70b4357f-d5c5-5946-9022-0602c4217a1d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050687}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415771} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415772} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions"} :step/id #uuid "6acb0f4b-5b56-5e4c-a728-4db2fd840bff" :step/math-academy-id 23747 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180387}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180492 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179738 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179600 :question/id #uuid "171fef73-d124-5aca-ad06-9f20ae5a087c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9535"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bed301c-59ac-5cb3-85e4-4aeaeebc15c2" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050666}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180018} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259805} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387629} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415773} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424622} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536874}] :knowledge-point/title "Establishing the Connection With Line Integrals of Scalar Fields"} :step/id #uuid "c7465601-d18a-5a90-bfe9-a27fe52c871f" :step/math-academy-id 35726 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180386}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180501 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180722 :tutorial/content "Line integrals of vector-valued functions share a fundamental connection with line integrals with respect to $x$ and $y.$\n\nConsider a vector field $\\mathbf F$ on $\\mathbb R^2$ with components $P$ and $Q\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j\n$$\n\nNow suppose that $C$ is a smooth curve on $\\mathbb R^2,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r (t) = x(t)\\,\\mathbf i + y(t)\\,\\mathbf j, \\qquad t\\in [a,b].\n$$\n\nThen, we can write the line integral of $\\mathbf F$ along $C$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}F(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\,i+Q(x(t),y(t))\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j)\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}(P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}P(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t+Q(x(t),y(t))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{C}P(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y)  \\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nAn analogous result holds for vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3.$ That is, \n\n$$\n\\int\\limits_C \\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r  = \\int\\limits_C P(x,y,z)\\,\\textrm d x + Q(x,y,z)  \\,\\textrm d y + R(x,y,z) \\,\\textrm d z\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = P(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf j + R(x,y,z)\\,\\mathbf k.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b4ca8cae-81df-5c55-90ac-839b063e78a6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5837 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "4246c091-bba6-587e-8d43-72e5c01a1e08" :step/math-academy-id 35769 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180492}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179857}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424625} :activity/id #uuid "c1a363ff-d156-5c04-b913-8176faf9c905" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049346 :topic/id #uuid "ed9d339c-fde4-545b-86b4-381d2f85203f" :topic/math-academy-id 3704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424623 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424622 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d6b3-ad5e-5dc6-8ebb-24366de741cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424604} {:answer/id #uuid "90b2b589-9e4a-50b5-aade-f9285dbfd609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}2{e}^{x+y+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424608} {:answer/id #uuid "91f4495b-123f-57a5-9de2-b6a7f1714f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424609} {:answer/id #uuid "adc05a61-f7ae-5052-9af4-0da99b3ef9f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{2(x+y+z)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424611} {:answer/id #uuid "ffa1700d-0795-5ad2-aa9c-b22297bf2101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424616}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424604} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef1db59-b0fd-505e-b25a-7a9041ecb1ef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424619}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f7d70870-3ade-5c38-9cda-96260cf64575" :question/math-academy-id "q-125414" :question/problem "Let $F(x,y,z)={e}^{y+z}\\,i+{e}^{x+z}\\,j+{e}^{x+y}\\,k$ and let $C$ be a path in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a piecewise-smooth path $C$ and a vector field \n\n$$\nF(x,y,z)=P(x,y,z)\\,i+Q(x,y,z)\\,j+R(x,y,z)\\,k\n$$\n\ndefined on $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{C}P(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x+Q(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y+R(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{C}{e}^{y+z}\\,\\text{d}x+{e}^{x+z}\\,\\text{d}y+{e}^{x+y}\\,\\text{d}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "325a719e-fd77-51b3-8a73-41ee2a5e0859"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424624 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424621 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b97760-1703-5852-afc4-69acd21e7cc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424603} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd6afbc-8569-51f5-a569-dd388d648b11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} {:answer/id #uuid "79d50006-6f06-56c3-bce2-b65f3e1eb21c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424607} {:answer/id #uuid "d37fe04f-4a91-5f51-9524-f7477c2a7f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424612} {:answer/id #uuid "e17522ae-4622-53a9-bb60-7bec4bbcce91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424613}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424605} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fdba31b-0f4f-583e-a52e-f0510ad8e3c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424617}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "515517e3-98f2-5a9f-ba03-8d3b47263ffd" :question/math-academy-id "q-77258" :question/problem "![](images/4d/4d4c2c86ed8506ff034141ac20a5a56587a80370384ceeaf08c66f2ce3e8a315.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a section of the parabola $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(4,-2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(4,-2)$ to the point $B(4,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{8}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the section of the curve $y=-\\sqrt{x}$ from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t)=t\\,i-\\sqrt{t}\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 4.$ Along this curve, we have $x=t$ and $y=-\\sqrt{t}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =x\\,i+{y}^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =t\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\sqrt{t})j \\\\  & =i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =(t\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j) \\\\  & =t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}(t-\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2})\\text{d}t+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{t}^{3/2}}{3}]}_{0}^{4}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{4}^{3/2}}{3}-0)+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}+\\frac{8}{3} \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7063d477-7b30-59c1-824d-924e55d3b439" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424623}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424625 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b359e4-02cd-5e97-8b05-35ed5f867cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424602} {:answer/id #uuid "65eebd17-cd71-544b-a441-a52714eabfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424606} {:answer/id #uuid "97231ff5-005f-5e53-bec5-aa0b22f5882f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424610} {:answer/id #uuid "e35dbb22-7632-5914-b08b-f1a1a963fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424614} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b66872-c5ce-599c-8fa3-26dd57b225b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615} :answer-field/id #uuid "d716774a-ff3b-5a8f-950b-7d77b8284d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77145" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "c29adf1a-cc66-5223-9397-edc496c85d37" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424624}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424601}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550100} :activity/id #uuid "fc8d5ef6-ad4d-51c7-8822-84767360e2eb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049346 :topic/id #uuid "ed9d339c-fde4-545b-86b4-381d2f85203f" :topic/math-academy-id 3704} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550035 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013ef795-942d-50f4-83dc-15aab4fbe0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342257} {:answer/id #uuid "3c789914-0f4b-5e38-a6be-25a97983968e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342736} {:answer/id #uuid "9c29e367-5a7d-5e08-a8c4-ffe4554e420a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343615} {:answer/id #uuid "b0325901-d312-55e8-994f-b53760b697b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343787} {:answer/id #uuid "c081d7e3-54be-5608-bc89-650e95c065c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342257} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c651ff-ec09-54bc-bcdf-8c29a3729b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a475ad3-f062-505d-a7c3-573f5f8f58ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-77131" :question/problem "![](images/54/5484647a9c694b95bd9e4b569c84f7263c5e69de5e98f12a1081fd937d298769.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $KMLK,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "271a47d9-c574-51a4-b4e3-461cef8bc255" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550110}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345284 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21279c87-66bb-50e4-8d0a-b4f4a20df582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342511} {:answer/id #uuid "56b72719-148e-5b84-8585-452f45bb273d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342960} {:answer/id #uuid "5c203805-aaee-5456-a95d-7a12caeeb4c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343009} {:answer/id #uuid "683b017e-1f61-5d0d-8485-fec00b137cb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343128} {:answer/id #uuid "fb4af7cc-9165-5222-ac1c-d3e9cde8c428" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344449}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342511} :answer-field/id #uuid "22e376b5-f6bc-5044-8715-29e828c6d70a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b2144499-6ba0-5eff-ada5-d16752f7b523" :question/math-academy-id "q-77182" :question/problem "![](images/60/6087da076e65bda5240c428ff67c570ad59ae9a8e5a5a3c9e8406184998de56a.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $O(0,0)$ to the point $A(1,0),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,0)$ to the point $B(0,-2).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{3},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=({x}^{2}+y)\\,i+(x-{y}^{2})\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the horizontal line segment from $O$ to $A.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =0+t(a-0) \\\\  & =⟨0,0⟩+t(⟨1,0⟩-⟨0,0⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,0⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=0.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =({x}^{2}+y)\\,i+(x-{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+0)\\,i+(t-{0}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)j \\\\  & =i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =({t}^{2}\\,i+t\\,j)\\cdot (i) \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{t}^{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3}-0)+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{7}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5b83a2d9-8217-5e31-a5fa-035a5c1103a2"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345283 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f99a0c2-ff39-555f-979d-c5dceb67d68c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342761} {:answer/id #uuid "561cf640-b717-5a0c-a5a7-05b94a807500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342954} {:answer/id #uuid "b645e1eb-5ce9-56ca-8d1b-c4fb928eafdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343836} {:answer/id #uuid "d687ba62-fd77-5fb2-85a6-257e3f469e25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344120} {:answer/id #uuid "ece5dfda-1ddb-554b-acda-daf0da60f92e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344318}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342761} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ab12311-2d02-5230-960f-45a14af856ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "616650f8-c315-5a80-80b8-1d1e65ec51b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-77180" :question/problem "![](images/93/93c6184f39b28a057866bb02e11e5e81a7abea4ff8a9a907670dd30cfc93d557.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a line segment from the point $A(1,-1)$ to the point $B(1,1),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,1)$ to the point $O(0,0).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the first integral, so we only need to work out the second.\n\nThe path ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment from $B$ to $O.$ It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}(t) & =b+t(0-b) \\\\  & =⟨1,1⟩+t(⟨0,0⟩-⟨1,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨1-t,1-t⟩ \\\\  & =(1-t)\\,i+(1-t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=1-t$ and $y=1-t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t)) & =(2x+y)\\,i+(x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2(1-t)+(1-t))\\,i+((1-t)-2(1-t))\\,j \\\\  & =(3-3t)\\,i+(-1+t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)j \\\\  & =-i-j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t) & =((3-3t)\\,i+(-1+t)\\,j)\\cdot (-i-j) \\\\  & =-3+3t+1-t \\\\  & =-2+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{2}(t))\\cdot {r}_{2}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+{\\int }_{0}^{1}(-2+2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2+(-2t+{t}^{2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+(1-2) \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "b0747b36-5013-549d-a28a-aa3dd9ab1385" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550100 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326775 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6cd156-8ca4-5855-a3cc-5f0d395f4aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324341} {:answer/id #uuid "317a4675-7255-5700-9ad9-8cfade1093ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324370} {:answer/id #uuid "68507ca4-abf8-5cd6-8193-9965c1c024dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324842} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a34229-1bb6-551f-bdc6-3c2c3f4134b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325504} {:answer/id #uuid "ceeccb5b-f4c0-5768-a9a9-bdc8a37556ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325768}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325504} :answer-field/id #uuid "01d160d2-e817-535b-93be-44d3599c92c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326223}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c31eda49-a231-50f2-81e8-83c448690ed2" :question/math-academy-id "q-39673" :question/problem "![](images/73/73c6a8268a179f72611fb65e099d1249d48501e4fd4731d03747800e1944fd97.png)\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ consists of a the line segment from the point $A(0,1)$ to the point $B(1,2),$ and the path ${C}_{2}$ consists of a line segment from the point $B(1,2)$ to the point $D(1,3).$\n\nGiven that ${\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=\\frac{7}{2},$ evaluate ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $F(x,y)=(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j,$ and $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the fact that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$\n\nWe're given the value of the second integral, so we only need to work out the first.\n\nThe path ${C}_{1}$ is the line segment from $A$ to $B.$  It can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}(t) & =a+t(b-a) \\\\  & =⟨0,1⟩+t(⟨1,2⟩-⟨0,1⟩) \\\\  & =⟨t,1+t⟩ \\\\  & =t\\,i+(1+t)\\,j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0<t<1.$ Along this segment, we have $x=t$ and $y=1+t.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t)) & =(2x-3y)\\,i+(x+y)\\,j \\\\  & =(2t-3(1+t))\\,i+(t+(1+t))\\,j \\\\  & =(-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivative, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)j \\\\  & =i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now compute the dot product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t) & =((-3-t)\\,i+(2t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =-3-t+2t+1 \\\\  & =t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the integral, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}F({r}_{1}(t))\\cdot {r}_{1}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(t-2)\\,\\text{d}t+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}-2t]}_{0}^{1}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{1}{2}-2-0]+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{7}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "bd8e2704-5f40-5faf-b9f0-5291baf681f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550035}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550110 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424620 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09b359e4-02cd-5e97-8b05-35ed5f867cf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424602} {:answer/id #uuid "65eebd17-cd71-544b-a441-a52714eabfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424606} {:answer/id #uuid "97231ff5-005f-5e53-bec5-aa0b22f5882f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424610} {:answer/id #uuid "e35dbb22-7632-5914-b08b-f1a1a963fdbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424614} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b66872-c5ce-599c-8fa3-26dd57b225b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186424615} :answer-field/id #uuid "d716774a-ff3b-5a8f-950b-7d77b8284d38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186424618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37832b23-49d2-5d4e-9f43-b03259f479e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77145" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3c874f3c4564795a2ddcfb4c2896c1bc0ef1b4b7b3d8e8318f42d15643942d7.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $UVWU,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the **clockwise** direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed clockwise, as the union of $3$ paths:\n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup \\{-{C}_{2}\\}\\cup \\{-{C}_{3}\\},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "def0e380-3b81-5346-998d-cfdab0e48044" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186550089}}] :activity/title "Properties of Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186549319}]}
